{
  "dom_id": "question-166364",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "Using a trapezoidal sum with two equal subintervals, ${\\int }_{0}^{4}q(t)\\,\\text{d}t≈$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n.",
  "worked_solution": "The general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}q(t)\\,\\text{d}t≈\\frac{1}{2}[q(a)+2(q({t}_{1})+q({t}_{2})+…+q({t}_{n-1}))+q(b)]Δt,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and $Δt=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ is the step size.\n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n- The number of trapezoids is $n=2.$\n\n- The corresponding interval is $[0,4],$ so we have $a=0$ and $b=4.$\n\n- The step size is given by\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{4-0}{2}=2.\n$$\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}q(t)\\,\\text{d}t≈\\frac{1}{2}[q(0)+2q(2)+q(4)]\\cdot 2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}q(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ≈\\frac{1}{2}[0+2(120)+108]\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =240+108 \\\\  & =348.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
